      cross_domain_range: [74, 0.56, "s", "A"],
      // [src_mach_dsb, src_mach_sep, src_mach_waal, src_mach_encsci | 1872-1913 | reference biography, scholarly encyclopedia, history-of-physics article, reference entry] He argued that Newton's absolute space and time could not be tied to observation and proposed relating motion to the other bodies of the universe (DSB, Stanford); he refused the atomic-molecular view even as physicists around him accepted it and set his descriptionist view against Planck's 1908 attack (DSB, de Waal and ten Hagen, reference entry); Einstein in 1946 praised his 'incorruptible skepticism and independence' (quoted by the DSB). The DSB adds that his stance was often judged hypercritical and obstreperous, and that the anti-atomism cost him credibility, hence dual-edged. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 82 / 0.66 to 78 / 0.60 because the Einstein tribute is a pupil-side admirer's retrospective view and the critique of mechanics and the anti-atomism are one theme. RUBRIC_CORRECTION (Roster67 lead review): 78@0.6 -> 74@0.56. the Newton-critique episode is retold across DSB, Stanford and an encyclopedia entry from one scholarly lineage; relativity-preface authorship is also contested.
      independent_thinking: [74, 0.56, "s", "D"],
      // [src_mach_dsb, src_mach_bluh, src_mach_sep, src_mach_waal | 1863-1913 | reference biography, historian-physicist's article, scholarly encyclopedia, history-of-physics article] The DSB and Bluh show he began by using the atomic-molecular theory as a working hypothesis (Compendium, 1863) but within a year treated it as a provisional tool, an early change of view; the DSB then says he stayed 'dogmatic' about anti-atomism even after turn-of-century evidence and softened only in tone, while the Stanford entry calls his position less straightforward and reports that he may have told Einstein in 1910 that atoms were acceptable as an organising hypothesis. Sources disagree, so the score stays below the midpoint with low confidence. His alleged rejection of relativity in the Optics foreword is not used because its authenticity is disputed. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): confidence lowered from 0.48 to 0.44 because the DSB and Stanford entries conflict on how far his anti-atomism softened; score not changed.
      belief_updating: [42, 0.44, "i", "D"],
      // [src_mach_dsb, src_mach_sep, src_mach_bluh | 1860-1893 | reference biography, scholarly encyclopedia, historian-physicist's article] The DSB and Stanford entries describe an apparatus for the Doppler effect (1860), a pulse-wave recorder, the rotating-disc experiments behind Mach bands, optical and photographic techniques for sound waves, spark waves and projectiles (1873-93) and the 1887 photographs with Salcher; Bluh adds demonstration apparatus built in his Prague laboratory. Einstein praised his 'extraordinary experimental talent' (DSB). SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 80 / 0.66 to 76 / 0.62 because the instances come largely from one biographical record and the 1887 experiments were carried out jointly with Salcher in Fiume (DSB), so his own hands-on share is not isolated. RUBRIC_CORRECTION (Roster67 lead review): 76@0.62 -> 74@0.56. experimental programme is documented in DSB and Stanford which repeat one chronology; independent corroboration is thin.
      experimentation: [74, 0.56, "s", "A"],
      // [src_mach_sep, src_mach_dsb, src_mach_bluh | 1873-1905 | scholarly encyclopedia, reference biography, historian-physicist's article] Two distinct stretches: the shock-wave programme ran from the 1870s to the 1880s and 1893 (DSB), and after the stroke that paralysed his right side he continued to study, write and experiment with Ludwig's help and completed Knowledge and Error in 1905 (Stanford; DSB says he resumed lecturing and writing), although Bluh notes he insisted on retirement in 1901 and that the illness handicapped revision of his views. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 72 / 0.56 to 68 / 0.50 because the post-stroke evidence is a few sentences and depended on his son's help.
      persistence: [68, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_mach_dsb, src_mach_sep | 1860-1864 | reference biography, scholarly encyclopedia] The DSB says that at Graz, with no promise of an institute or equipment, he used his own funds to secure research facilities, and both the DSB and the Stanford entry report his own recollection that he turned to sensory physiology because it could be investigated without costly physical equipment. The second instance is his own later account reported by two writers. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 66 / 0.52 / strong_inference to 60 / 0.44 / inference because one fact comes from a single DSB sentence and the other is Mach's self-report.
      resourcefulness: [60, 0.44, "i", "A"],
