      cross_domain_range: [56, 0.46, "i", "A"],
      // [src_nagaoka_dsb, src_nagaoka_badash, src_nagaoka_baily | 1902-1911 | reference biography, historian's edition of his own letter, physics-history article] The DSB says his criticism of Kelvin's 1902 Aepinus atom was essential to forming his model, in which he rejected interpenetrating charges and placed the electrons outside a central positive charge; in 1910 he wrote that most of his opinions on earthquakes accorded with Benndorf's and differed from those of Japanese seismologists (his own letter). Baily records that the model contained sign errors and had no direct influence on Rutherford, so the independence did not make the work reliable. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 62 / 0.46 to 58 / 0.42 because the second instance is his own remark.
