    sources: [{ id: "src_wilkins_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Hubert Wilkins (Q577806), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-13): English label 'Hubert Wilkins', description 'Australian polar explorer (1888-1958)', Korean label 휴버트 윌킨스, born 1888-10-31, died 1958-11-30, instance of human, citizen of Australia. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q577806" }, { id: "src_wilkins_wikipedia", kind: "wikipedia", title: "English Wikipedia, 'Hubert Wilkins' (read for orientation and to locate the independent sources below). Not used as support for any row or card.", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hubert_Wilkins" }, { id: "src_wilkins_nasm", kind: "institution", title: "Mark Piesing, 'What Made the Lockheed Vega Such a Great Polar Explorer?', Air & Space Quarterly, National Air and Space Museum (Smithsonian), 30 September 2026 (read in full; draws on the Wilkins biographer Jeff Maynard and museum curators). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: the 1926 Detroit-sponsored flights that ended in wrecked Fokkers and a Stinson forced down on the sea ice, the switch to a single Lockheed Vega, the 15 April 1928 flight from Point Barrow to Spitsbergen (about 2,200 miles in some 20 hours, the first airplane crossing of the Arctic Ocean, which found sea ice and no land in the unexplored area), the four days trapped on Deadman's Island and the hand-dug runway, the knighthood, the Wilkins-Hearst Antarctic Expedition with the first powered flight in Antarctica on 16 November 1928 and a 600-mile survey flight of Graham Land on 20 December, the failed plan to fly on to the Ross Sea when the Deception Island runway melted, his Military Cross and bar, his request that General Monash not nominate him for the Victoria Cross, and his weather-station ambition. A museum feature that is admiring in tone.", url: "https://airandspace.si.edu/air-and-space-quarterly/issue-20/first-arctic-aerial-exploration" }, { id: "src_wilkins_mcconnell", kind: "biography", title: "Burt M. McConnell, 'Sir Hubert Wilkins', Encyclopedia Arctica vol. 15 (Biographies), unpublished reference work 1947-51, Rauner Special Collections, Dartmouth (read in full). A colleague's biography: McConnell served on the Canadian Arctic Expedition with Wilkins, so it is a first-hand witness for 1913-16 (Wilkins repairing a motorboat engine, taking his turn as cook, taking no part in the staff protest at Nome, staying loyal to Stefansson's ice journey, wintering on Banks Island, walking to Coronation Gulf for the schooner) and a secondhand summary afterwards (Balkan war films, Western Front, the Quest, the two-year British Museum collecting expedition with more than 5,000 specimens, the 1925 Detroit proposal, the 1927 forced landing and the 13-day walk ashore, the 1928 navigation preparations and flight, the Antarctic flights, the Nautilus, the 1937-38 search for Levanevsky). Admiring, written by an associate of Stefansson's circle; it also records that some Antarctic 'channels' reported in 1928 proved to be glaciers.", url: "https://collections.dartmouth.edu/arctica-beta/html/EA15-77.html" }, { id: "src_wilkins_historytrust", kind: "institution", title: "History Trust of South Australia, Sir Hubert Wilkins Project, timeline pages '1888-1916: Early Life and Cinematography', '1917-1919: Cameraman at the Western Front' and '1926-1931: Polar Aviator and Submariner' (all read in full). A state history museum's account with quotations from Wilkins's own writing: the Balkan war filming in 1912, the Canadian Arctic Expedition and how he learned to travel light from the Inuit, the Western Front work with Bean (the Military Cross for bringing wounded back from No Man's Land), the Detroit expedition and its crashes, the 1928 flights, Hearst's sponsorship of the submarine, the 'mutinous crew', and how he put his entire savings into the Nautilus and was financially ruined and immediately looked for new sponsors. Its statement that the Military Cross bar was for a charge on a machine-gun nest differs from the Australian War Memorial's account.", url: "https://www.history.sa.gov.au/wilkins-project/polar-aviator-and-submariner/" }, { id: "src_wilkins_awm", kind: "institution", title: "Australian War Memorial, 'Hubert Wilkins (1888-1958)' (Focus: photographers) and 'Captured in colour: George Hubert Wilkins 1888-1958' (both read in full). A national memorial's account of his work as official photographer: appointed in August 1917 after colour-blindness kept him from flying, 'famous for his bravery and recklessness' on the battlefield, the first person to film combat in the 1912 Balkan war, two Military Crosses (the first for bringing in wounded under fire, the bar for leading inexperienced American troops out of a deadly situation in September 1918), more than two years at the front, and the 1919 Gallipoli mission with Bean.", url: "https://www.awm.gov.au/visit/exhibitions/focus/hubert-wilkins" }, { id: "src_wilkins_aps", kind: "institution", title: "American Philosophical Society Library, online exhibition 'Low Road North: Sir Hubert Wilkins's 1931 Polar Expedition' (exhibition page and the 'History of the Expedition' page read in full; built on the scrapbook of the Nautilus's chief electrician Frank Blumberg). The Nautilus voyage: the plan agreed with Lincoln Ellsworth, the former U.S. Navy O-12 refitted and renamed, the start two months behind schedule under mounting criticism, the engine failures and tow by USS Wyoming, the lost diving rudders and the suspicion of sabotage tied to some crew's lack of confidence in Wilkins, his determination to carry out the science anyway, the first dives beneath a three-foot floe on 31 August, his being persuaded that it was no longer safe, and the scuttling on 20 November 1931. Says a late start and repeated mechanical problems prevented the Pole.", url: "https://www.amphilsoc.org/exhibits/nautilus/history.htm" }, { id: "src_wilkins_eoas", kind: "institution", title: "Encyclopedia of Australian Science and Innovation, 'Wilkins, George Hubert' (Swinburne University of Technology and University of Melbourne eScholarship; read in full). An institutional chronology that gives dated posts (Gaumont cameraman 1908-12, Canadian Arctic Expedition second-in-command 1913-16, Military Cross and bar 1918, naturalist on the Quest 1921-22, the 1923-25 British Museum collecting in tropical Australia, the 1928 trans-Arctic flight and knighthood, aerial Antarctic expeditions 1928-39, the 1931 submarine attempt, wartime consulting for the U.S. Army) and lists the biographies (Grierson 1960, Nasht 2006, the Australian Dictionary of Biography entry by Swan).", url: "https://www.eoas.info/biogs/P002755b.htm" }, { id: "src_wilkins_ageo", kind: "press", title: "Jeff Maynard, 'Australia's unsung hero', Australian Geographic, 11 January 2024 (read in full). A magazine essay by Wilkins's biographer (the same informant as the National Air and Space Museum feature, so not independent of it): General Monash naming him the bravest man under his command and calling him 'aggressively modest' when he asked not to be put up for the Victoria Cross, his refusal to carry a gun, nine Antarctic visits, the mutinous Nautilus crew, his ruin after 1931, the Australian government's refusal in 1939 to back his Antarctic plan at Mawson's urging, and the author's own warning that biographers meet conflicting information and fanciful tales about him. Used for context and for the limits of the evidence.", url: "https://www.australiangeographic.com.au/history-culture/2024/01/australias-unsung-hero/" }],
