      resourcefulness: [64, 0.44, "i", "A"],
      // [src_singer_cooper, src_singer_ebsco, src_singer_lindahall | 1850 | curator history, encyclopedia entry, library essay] Cooper says that when the carving machine drew little interest he turned to the sewing machine as a device offering considerable opportunity for improvement and financial reward; EBSCO says he first refused Phelps's request, thinking the machine beneath him, so sources differ on who proposed it; Linda Hall says he 'stumbled into' sewing machines and credits Clark and Potter for the commercial insights. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 66 / 0.46 to 60 / 0.38 because the sources disagree on whether the pivot was his own idea and the later commercial foresight is credited to others.
      opportunity_sensing: [60, 0.38, "i", "A"],
      // [src_singer_gale, src_singer_ebsco, src_singer_cooper | 1839-1850 | reference profile, encyclopedia entry, curator history] He spent the proceeds of his $2,000 patent sale on founding his own acting company, which ran out of money (Gale, EBSCO), and arrived in Boston in 1850 with no money, relying on a backer's forty dollars for the sewing-machine model (Cooper). Both episodes are documented as financial exposure; no source describes how he weighed the odds. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 64 / 0.46 to 60 / 0.40 because the acting-company episode is told in the same few reference lines in two sources.
      risk_tolerance: [60, 0.4, "i", "D"],
      // [src_singer_cooper, src_singer_lindahall, src_singer_ebsco | 1851-1858 | curator history, library essay, encyclopedia entry] Cooper says Singer enjoyed demonstrating the machine to church and social groups and even at circuses and that his aggressive selling helped overcome the public's suspicion of sewing machines; EBSCO credits his flair for the dramatic in selling it, but Linda Hall and the company history credit Clark with the door-to-door selling, showrooms and instalment plan. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 62 / 0.46 to 58 / 0.40 because selling credit is divided between Singer and Clark and the EBSCO characterisation is a biographer's summary.
      persuasiveness: [58, 0.4, "i", "A"],
      // [src_singer_cooper, src_singer_funding, src_singer_ebsco | 1850-1863 | curator history, company history, encyclopedia entry] He worked with a series of partners: Phelps and Zieber on the first machines (Cooper), Clark from the Howe suit onward (Cooper, company history), and EBSCO says he surrounded himself with lawyers and bankers who could help him. The partnership with Clark was dissolved in 1863 after the firm's incorporation, and the sources differ on its start date and terms, so the relationship pattern is documented but not its quality. Reviewed in self-calibration and kept.
      collaboration: [56, 0.44, "i", "D"],
      // [src_singer_cooper, src_singer_gale, src_singer_funding | 1854-1856 | curator history, reference profile, company history] After a court order stopped sales of his machines while the Howe case was pending, he and Clark paid Howe $15,000 for a licence on 1 July 1854 instead of continuing the fight, and in 1856 his firm joined the patent pool (Cooper; Gale gives the same outcome). The company history credits Clark with the settlements and the pool. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 60 / 0.44 to 56 / 0.38 because the decision is described as joint with Clark and the pool is credited to Potter and Clark.
      adaptability: [56, 0.38, "i", "A"],
      // [src_singer_cooper, src_singer_gale | 1850-1851 | curator history, reference profile] He came to Boston to promote his carving machine himself, bought out Phelps's interest in the new sewing-machine company in late December 1850 (Cooper) and kept improving the machine and demonstrating it in public; Gale lists three patents in 1852-54. The episodes overlap with the persistence and resourcefulness rows and all come from one family of sources. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 64 / 0.50 strong_inference to 60 / 0.44 inference because the episodes overlap with other rows and rest mostly on Cooper.
      proactive_agency: [60, 0.44, "i", "A"],
