    sources: [{ id: "src_lesya_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Lesya Ukrainka (Q298033), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-13): English label 'Lesya Ukrainka', description 'Ukrainian poet, writer and feminist (1871-1913)', instance of human, born 13 February (Julian) / 25 February 1871, died 1 August 1913, Korean label 레샤 우크라인카. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q298033" }, { id: "src_lesya_eou", kind: "biography", title: "Petro Odarchenko, 'Ukrainka, Lesia', Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine (Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies; first printed in the Encyclopedia of Ukraine, vol. 5, 1993; read in full with its bibliography). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: poet and playwright (pseudonym of Larysa Kosach-Kvitka, 1871-1913) who reached her literary heights in the 'dramatic poem', a genre she developed, beginning with Oderzhyma (1901); her best-known dramatic works include The Stone Host, Cassandra, The Boyar Woman, In the Catacombs and the neoromantic Forest Song (1911); first poems in Zoria (Lviv, 1884), collections Na krylakh pisen (1893), Dumy i mriyi (1899), Vidhuky (1902); translations of Gogol, Mickiewicz, Heine, Hugo and Homer, the Heine Book of Songs translated with Maksym Slavinsky (1892); self-educated, a history textbook for her sisters at 19; member of the Pleiada literary group in Kyiv; the Babylonian-captivity dramatic poems read as symbols of Ukrainian subjection. Scholarly reference entry by a literary historian, admiring in tone; the illness it also notes is not used for any row.", url: "https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CU%5CK%5CUkrainkaLesia.htm" }, { id: "src_lesya_languagelanterns", kind: "biography", title: "Roma Franko (sketch), edited by Sonia Morris, 'Lesya Ukrainka (1871-1913): Biographical Sketch', Language Lanterns Publications, 1998 (publisher's page, read in full). Educational biography: Ukrainian-language home education by tutors, languages learned (Russian, Polish, Bulgarian, Greek, Latin, French, Italian, German, English), the Pleiades circle organised with her brother Mykhailo at seventeen, her uncle Mykhailo Drahomanov encouraging her to collect folk songs and study history and the Bible, the Heine translation (1892) and first collection (1893) printed in Western Ukraine and smuggled into Kyiv because Ukrainian publications were banned in the Russian Empire, her correspondence with Olha Kobylianska, marriage in 1907 to the ethnographer Klyment Kvitka who transcribed and published the folk songs she knew. Publisher biography for a translation series.", url: "https://www.languagelanterns.com/ukrainka.htm" }, { id: "src_lesya_euromaidan", kind: "press", title: "Christine Chraibi, 'The many faces of Ukrainian writer & feminist Lesia Ukrainka', Euromaidan Press, 25 March 2020 (read in full). Popular online biography: the Pleiada circle founded in 1888 with her brother, her membership in the Kyiv Literary and Artistic Society (1895-97), her collecting and performing of folk songs, a history manual written at 19, one impromptu poem in Russian, her marriage in 1907 to Klyment Kvitka against her mother's opposition after renouncing family financial help (single-source claim), and her 1891 letter from Vienna on Austrian freedom compared with the Russian Empire. Popular-press source that repeats family-memoir material; some health details in it are not used.", url: "https://euromaidanpress.com/2020/03/25/the-many-faces-of-ukrainian-writer-feminist-lesia-ukrainka/" }, { id: "src_lesya_radiosvoboda", kind: "press", title: "Halyna Tereshchuk, 'Dvi lyubovni dramy: u Lvovi prezentuvaly fotohrafiyu Lesi Ukrainky 1901 roku' (Radio Svoboda, 25 February 2021; read in full, in Ukrainian). Report on the exhibition of her 1901 Chernivtsi photograph at the Ivan Franko literary-memorial museum in Lviv (with comments from the museum director Bohdan Tykholoz, the researcher Tamara Skrypka and her husband Klyment Kvitka's recollection that she found the photograph truest to her) and on Ivan Trush's 1900 portrait of her: sittings in Kyiv for the Shevchenko Scientific Society, Trush's sale of the replica to Count Leon Pininsky, her insistence in 1903 that the replica be destroyed, and her breaking off contact with him when he refused. Journalism with museum-side voices.", url: "https://www.radiosvoboda.org/a/oryginalna-istoriya-svitlyna-lesi-ukrayinky/31121801.html" }, { id: "src_lesya_portal", kind: "institution", title: "Lesya Ukrainka Portal (lesyaukrainka.com), entry 'Trush I. Lesya Ukrainka (portrait)' in the section on Lesya Ukrainka in the visual arts (read in full, in Ukrainian). Research-portal note: the 1900 portrait painted by Ivan Trush as part of the Shevchenko Scientific Society's gallery of Ukrainian figures, sittings in late March and early April 1900 in a temporary studio in the Kyiv museum, first shown in Lviv in June 1901, her guarded reaction, the dispute over the unauthorised sale and the replica; says one version is now in the National Museum in Lviv and the other in the National Art Museum of Ukraine in Kyiv. Used for the portrait record and the 1903 dispute; shares its account with the Radio Svoboda report.", url: "https://lesyaukrainka.com/ua/mistectvo/lesya-ukrayinka-v-obrazotvorchomu-mistectvi/1_trush-i--lesya-ukrayinka--portret-" }],
