      mastery_orientation: [72, 0.56, "s", "A"],
      // [src_cassatt_salem, src_cassatt_voves, src_cassatt_spectator, src_cassatt_artnews | 1878-1891 | reference biography, exhibition reviews] Three distinct documented departures: turning to pastels and prints on Degas's urging (Salem), the colour aquatints inspired by the 1890 Japanese print exhibition, pursued as a set of ten (Voves, Art Newspaper), and the planned journal of engravings 'Le Jour et la nuit' with Degas, Pissarro and Bracquemond, which was never published (Voves). The Spectator says she was the first to combine pastel with metallic paint, hedged with 'apparently'. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 68 / 0.52 to 64 / 0.50 because the 'first' claim is hedged and three of the four sources are reviews of the same exhibition.
      experimentation: [64, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_cassatt_wiwh, src_cassatt_salem, src_cassatt_voves, src_cassatt_natreview | 1877-1904 | encyclopedia biography, reference biography, exhibition reviews] She said that joining the Impressionists meant she could 'work with absolute independence without considering the opinion of a jury' (Salem; the same words recur in other accounts); she stopped sending work to the Salon after 1877 (Salem, Ojala), declined the 1904 Lippincott and Harris prizes on the principle 'no jury, no medals, no awards' (Ojala) and wrote that she was independent and could live alone (Voves). The National Review qualifies the picture: she had a family allowance and borrowed from her dealer to buy a house. CONCENTRATION (agent): lowered from 76 / 0.58 to 72 / 0.52 because the strongest statements are her own words, repeated across sources, and the prize refusal is documented by one encyclopedia entry.
      autonomy_need: [72, 0.52, "s", "A"],
      // [src_cassatt_wiwh, src_cassatt_salem | 1877-1913 | encyclopedia biography, reference biography] She left the Salon system for a group the critics ridiculed, and in the Dreyfus Affair she slowly came to doubt his guilt while Degas would not, whereas in later life she dismissed the Steins' modern collection and Monet's water lilies in sharp terms (Ojala). The same independence of judgement ran against the new art of her time, hence dual-edged. CONCENTRATION (agent): lowered from 70 / 0.54 to 66 / 0.48 and from strong_inference to inference because both sources repeat the same joining-the-Impressionists statement and the later episodes come from a single entry.
      independent_thinking: [66, 0.48, "i", "D"],
      // [src_cassatt_wiwh, src_cassatt_spectator, src_cassatt_voves | 1870s-1900s | encyclopedia biography, exhibition reviews] The encyclopedia calls her overtly ambitious and eager for recognition and financial security; the Spectator quotes her telling a friend she was 'ravenous' for money; Voves says she was driven to succeed commercially, and she organised one-woman shows in Paris. Against this she refused prizes and a state commission offer (Ojala), so the drive is better read as drive for her own professional standing than for honours, hence dual-edged. CONCENTRATION (agent): lowered from 68 / 0.52 to 64 / 0.46 and from strong_inference to inference because the ambition is documented by one biographer's characterisation and her own remark, with the refusals pointing the other way.
