      proactive_agency: [62, 0.46, "i", "A"],
      // [src_sverdrup_snl, src_sverdrup_barr, src_sverdrup_fram, src_sverdrup_nansen | 1895-1902 | national reference biography, polar-historian profile, museum history, first-hand account] Nansen handed him written command of the Fram on 25 February 1895, and he was in sole charge of ship and crew until August 1896 (Nansen, Barr); he then led the second Fram expedition himself and began planning it immediately (Barr, Harper). The museum says he was 'urged' to lead it and the reference article says Nansen first suggested a new voyage, and every source calls him quiet and workmanlike, so drive is inferred from the roles he took, not from stated ambition. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 64 / 0.50 to 60 / 0.44 because the roles were offered to him as much as sought.
      leadership_drive: [60, 0.44, "i", "A"],
      // [src_sverdrup_snl, src_sverdrup_barr, src_sverdrup_nansen, src_sverdrup_lifeinnorway | 1888-1897 | national reference biography, polar-historian profile, first-hand account, popular article] He crossed Greenland and made the Godthab boat journey with Nansen and served as his deputy for three years (Nansen, reference article), and Barr calls his sledging team 'well-knit' and well coordinated. Against that, the reference article records a dispute with Nansen over book rights and friction because the scientists were placed under the second-in-command rather than under him, and the popular article reports Nansen accusing him in 1897 of going behind his back in fund-raising. Reviewed in self-calibration and kept.
