      // [src_wali_koc, src_wali_fielding, src_wali_essays | 1858-1859 | journal article, scholar's essay, own account] He travelled to Kashgar in 1858-59 under a false merchant identity and name (Alimbay), in a region just emerging from a revolt, a year after the German geographer Adolph Schlagintweit had been executed there (Koc); Fielding calls it a year-long undercover expedition in one of the most remote Qing outposts, and his own text shows him declining to describe the interrogations of the authorities. The mission was proposed by Semyonov and ordered by the governor-general rather than chosen by him alone, and he was young and well supported, so the lean is moderate. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 76 / 0.58 to 68 / 0.5 because it rests on one ordered mission, described by sources that share a tradition, and the sources do not show how he himself weighed the danger.
      risk_tolerance: [68, 0.5, "s", "D"],
      // [src_wali_koc, src_wali_essays, src_wali_fielding | 1858-1861 | journal article, own account, scholar's essay] He passed as a Kokandi merchant's relative for months among Uighur and Kokandi traders, moving between steppe, Kyrgyz and Qing-Turkestan settings (Koc, his own text), and later worked in the Russian capital's map office and learned societies while remaining, in Fielding's account, an outsider there. The adaptation is documented in one episode and in his education across two cultures. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 72 / 0.55 (strong_inference) to 66 / 0.48 (inference) because the best-documented instance is the single disguise episode and the later claims are Fielding's characterisation.
      adaptability: [66, 0.48, "i", "A"],
      // [src_wali_fielding, src_wali_koc, src_wali_ehistory | 1862-1864 | scholar's essay, journal article, national history portal] In 1864 he joined General Chernyaev's southward expedition but, according to Fielding and Koc, left the army in protest at the treatment of the local population (the portal says he returned with a group of dissatisfied officers); Fielding quoting Winner says he wrote memoranda criticising Russian policy toward the Kazakhs and favoured a more egalitarian approach than the tribal hierarchy, and he stood for the post of elder sultan in 1862. Koc adds that he first took part in the Russian capture of the Aulie-Ata fortress before leaving, so the protest followed participation. CONCENTRATION CORRECTION (agent): lowered from 74 / 0.6 to 66 / 0.5 because the three sources describe the same 1864 episode from one biographical tradition and the memoranda come through a single quotation.
      independent_thinking: [66, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_wali_fielding, src_wali_koc, src_wali_essays | 1859-1860 | scholar's essay, journal article, own account] Fielding calls the Kashgar report short, packed with reliable information from climate and flora to topography; Koc reports its detailed description of Qing administrative titles and measures; his own essay records everyday detail and treats rumours sceptically. The evidence is the quality of his reports, not a documented working routine. CONCENTRATION CORRECTION (agent): lowered from 68 / 0.52 (strong_inference) to 60 / 0.44 (inference) because the claim comes from admiring descriptions of one output and Plumtree's study of his Manas work shows he also altered and omitted details.
      detail_orientation: [60, 0.44, "i", "A"],
      // [src_wali_fielding, src_wali_ehistory, src_wali_koc | 1856-1864 | scholar's essay, national history portal, journal article] He worked in ethnography and folklore (Manas, Kazakh epics), history and genealogy of the khans, religion (shamanism, Islam in the steppe, history of Buddhism), geography and natural history (collections and herbarium, survey of Issyk-Kul), cartography (maps of Central Asia for the General Staff), Qing administration and Kazakh customary law. Many of the fields served one programme of describing Central Asia for the Russian state and for his own people. CONCENTRATION CORRECTION (agent): lowered from 74 / 0.58 to 68 / 0.5 because the list comes mostly from one biographical tradition and the fields overlap.
      cross_domain_range: [68, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_wali_ehistory, src_wali_fielding | 1862-1864 | national history portal, scholar's essay] He stood in the local election for elder sultan of Atbasar in 1862 (Fielding says corrupt officials thwarted him; the portal says he aimed to overcome backwardness and lawlessness), then worked on the legal commission and on judicial reform of the steppe. Much of his other work was assigned by superiors. CONCENTRATION CORRECTION (agent): lowered from 66 / 0.5 to 60 / 0.44 (strong_inference to inference) because the self-started actions are only the election bid and the later reform work, thinly sourced and partly government commissioned.
