      // [src_steinitz_cbfischer, src_steinitz_gale, src_steinitz_wintericm | 1873-1891 | chess-news biography quoting two modern authors, reference biography, magazine quotations] The ChessBase biography (quoting Pritchett) says he and William Potter spent countless hours on the 1873-74 London-Vienna correspondence games and annotated them exhaustively, and that Steinitz wrote of 'the systematic analytical development of modern ideas'; Gale says his Field annotations were a vast improvement on earlier ones and that he annotated each of the 432 games of the Sixth American Chess Congress book; in 1886 he pointed to the opening novelties in the Zukertort match games and in 1891 wrote that only while writing his book was he going through the openings systematically. The correspondence analysis rests on one quoted author and the rest on Gale and his own statements. CONCENTRATION CHECK (agent): lowered from 74 / 0.62 to 70 / 0.54 because the claim rests on one quoted author, one reference entry and his own statements.
      analytical_rigor: [70, 0.54, "s", "A"],
      // [src_steinitz_wintericm, src_steinitz_gale, src_steinitz_hastings | 1885-1895 | magazine quotations, reference biography, tournament book] In his own magazine he disputed the picture of Morphy as all inspiration (July 1886: Morphy 'never in his practice introduced a single novelty', whereas modern masters had to study) and Anderssen's belief in Morphy's superiority (February 1885); Gale says contemporaries found his style crabbed and unaesthetic yet it prevailed; the Hastings book notes eccentric opening choices that may come from 'self-reliance'. Documented as a willingness to argue against prevailing opinion, mostly in his own words. CONCENTRATION CHECK (agent): lowered from 72 / 0.56 to 68 / 0.5 because the strongest evidence is his own writing and a historian's selection of it.
      independent_thinking: [68, 0.5, "s", "A"],
