      belief_updating: [58, 0.4, "i", "A"],
      // [src_steinitz_gale, src_steinitz_cbmanlapao, src_steinitz_cbfischer, src_steinitz_winterlasker | 1882-1896 | reference biography, chess-history article, chess-news biography, historian's compilation] After losing four of the first five games of the 1886 match he won ten to five (Gale, Manlapao, who quotes a contemporary on how he kept playing even when behind); he returned to tournament play in 1882 after nine years away (ChessBase); he challenged Lasker to a rematch immediately after the 1894 loss, wrote on 10 October 1894 that Lasker had forfeited the title by breaking the agreed rematch (Winter), and then played the 1896-97 rematch. Several episodes, but all end in the chess-history storyline and winning the 1886 comeback invites outcome bias. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 68 / 0.54 to 66 / 0.5 because the best-known episode is a winning comeback and the rest are title disputes.
